  "rationale": "Across all four window tasks the same failure mode repeats: the student reads 5-10 files, never applies a single edit (edits: 0 applied / 0 failed), and then declares itself finished while tests still fail (stopReason 'done' at only 1 test run). The control gap is that no routine ever forces a concrete patch or verifies it. I add three small, generic routines that run before the fallback: (1) baseline-run does one deterministic full test run so we get structured failures without spending a student call; (2) locate-and-patch deterministically picks the innermost non-test source frame of a failure, reads a short excerpt around it, asks the student one focused 'patch' question and applies the resulting search/replace; (3) verify-and-decide re-runs the full suite, marks the task done only when it is green, and reverts a patch that increases the failure count. Caps and a 'tried' set keep the forced-patch loop bounded so the immutable ask-student fallback still gets budget. This targets the shared control behaviour (never committing to an edit, never verifying) rather than any package-specific fix.",
